  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Russula silvicola, commonly known as the emetic russula, is a small to medium-sized brittlegill mushroom characterized by its fragile flesh and tendency to cause gastrointestinal distress if consumed. Despite its toxic properties, it plays an important ecological role as a mycorrhizal fungus in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap color and texture",
      "description": "The cap is typically whitish to pale gray or cream-colored, with a slightly sticky surface when moist and a diameter of 2-4 inches.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills and spore print",
      "description": "White or pale cream-colored gills that are closely spaced, with a white spore print that is diagnostic for this species group.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Taste test (spit out)",
      "description": "The flesh tastes distinctly peppery or acrid when nibbled and immediately spit out, a classic field identification method for emetic russulas.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Russula silvicola is found in mixed and coniferous forests throughout New England, forming mycorrhizal relationships with pine, fir, and other trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name Russula comes from the Latin word for 'reddish,' yet many species like R. silvicola are white or pale, and the emetic compounds they contain may actually serve to protect the fungus from being eaten by insects and small animals.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
